description:
pkix-chain/src/lib.rs lines 1521-1534:

    match eku.0.as_slice() {
        [oid] if *oid == ID_KP_TIME_STAMPING => Ok(()),
        [_] => Err(Error::ProfileViolation {
            reason: Cow::Borrowed(
                "TSA ExtendedKeyUsage must contain only id-kp-timeStamping (RFC 3161 §2.3)",
            ),
        }),
        _ => Err(Error::ProfileViolation {
            reason: Cow::Borrowed(
                "TSA ExtendedKeyUsage must contain only id-kp-timeStamping (RFC 3161 §2.3)",
            ),
        }),
    }

The [_] and _ arms produce IDENTICAL ProfileViolation values. The two arms differ only structurally (the EKU has exactly one wrong OID vs more than one); the produced error is byte-identical.

SUGGESTED REWRITE:

    match eku.0.as_slice() {
        [oid] if *oid == ID_KP_TIME_STAMPING => Ok(()),
        _ => Err(Error::ProfileViolation {
            reason: Cow::Borrowed(
                "TSA ExtendedKeyUsage must contain only id-kp-timeStamping (RFC 3161 §2.3)",
            ),
        }),
    }

The single wildcard arm covers '0 OIDs', '1 wrong OID', '2+ OIDs (any combination)' identically and produces the same error — which is what the existing code does in two arms anyway.

If a future enhancement wants to distinguish 'wrong sole OID' from 'multiple OIDs' in the error message, that's an additive change; collapsing now does not prevent it.
